{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>At the end of seven years, at the appointed time of the year of release, at the festival of Sukkot.</b> This is the commandment of assembly (hakhel), and the meaning of מקץ is 'from the end'; and so every expression of קץ means 'end,' as in (Genesis 6): ״קץ כל בשר בא לפני״. And the intention is at the end of the seven years, that is, after the seven years have passed completely, which is the eighth day, in the intermediate days of the festival of Sukkot. But what is written concerning the commandment of release (Deuteronomy 15): ״מקץ שבע שנים תעשה שמטה״ — the meaning is 'from the end of seven years,' and this end is included within the seven, not that the seven have passed. And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: the seventh year does not release except at the end, which is the sun's setting at the conclusion of the seventh year. If so, the קץ mentioned here and the one mentioned in connection with release are the same — both are expressions of 'end' — except that the קץ mentioned here is outside the count of the seven, while the one mentioned in connection with release is included within the seven." "<b>And if</b> you gain insight into the secret of the commandment of assembly (hakhel), you will find that just as the commandment of release (shemittah) comes to allude to 'and one destroyed' (וחד חריב), so the commandment of assembly (hakhel), which is after the seven have passed completely at the beginning of the eighth, alludes to the matter of 'to make' (לעשות), through which the act of creation was sealed after He mentioned the matter of the Sabbath. And likewise we find (Psalms 92) in 'A Psalm, a song for the Sabbath day' that after it he mentions ״צדיק כתמר יפרח״ — and I have already mentioned this in Genesis. And the secret of assembly (hakhel) is that all beings in their entirety will be gathered and called before the King, HaShem; therefore its commandment was performed by the king, for the king would read from the Torah. And so the masters of truth said: the Holy One, blessed be He, would look into the Torah and build His world. And further, the reading of the Torah is to show that it is the sustenance of the world, and it is impossible for it to be created without it. And the assembly took place 'in the place that He would choose,' for the beginning of creation was from there, as is said (Psalms 50): ״מציון מכלל יופי״ — from Zion the world was perfected, and this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {1 "The Aramaic phrase 'וחד חריב' (literally 'and one destroyed') is a Kabbalistic allusion from Ramban's school; transliterated and left cryptic per voice rules."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:41.751628690Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}